  "summary": "A psalm is a poem or song addressed to God or about God. The Psalter includes wisdom teaching (Psalm 1), lament that turns toward praise (Psalm 22), trust (Psalm 23), royal praise (Psalm 110), and thanksgiving (Psalm 118). These different forms let people pray honestly through danger and joy, and they also became Scripture that later biblical writers quoted and interpreted.",
  "historical_context": "The Psalms preserve voices from different situations rather than one single occasion of worship. Psalm 22 gives words to someone who feels abandoned and later praises God; Psalm 23 speaks of trust in God as shepherd; Psalm 118 celebrates deliverance. Psalm 1 stands at the entrance to the collection and presents meditation on God’s instruction as a way of life. The setting of each poem shapes how its words are heard.",
  "ancient_near_east_context": "The cited psalms themselves show the range of Israel’s prayer and song: wisdom teaching, lament, trust, royal language, and thanksgiving. Since the record does not include comparative sources on ancient poetry, no claim about how Psalms differ from other ancient prayer traditions is needed to explain these examples.",
