  "literary_context": "The Psalter is a collection of poems, not one continuous narrative. Psalm 1 offers a wisdom frame; Psalm 22 moves from distress toward praise; Psalm 23 uses a shepherd image for trust; and Psalms 110 and 118 use royal and thanksgiving language. Luke 24, Acts 2, and Hebrews 1 later quote or interpret particular psalms in their arguments about Jesus.",
  "covenantal_significance": "The Psalms give worshippers words for remembering God’s care, asking for help, praising deliverance, and trusting through danger. Psalm 22 shows that lament can speak openly about suffering and still turn toward praise; Psalm 23 voices confidence in God’s guidance; Psalm 118 celebrates rescue. The poems model faithful speech without requiring every prayer to sound the same.",
  "authorship_positions": [],
  "date_ranges": [],
  "original_audience": "",
  "historical_setting": "",
  "canonical_role": "The Psalms function both as prayers in their own right and as Scripture later writers interpret. Luke 24 says Jesus opened the disciples’ understanding of the Psalms; Acts 2 cites Psalms 16 and 110; Hebrews 1 strings together psalm quotations about the Son. Those later readings add canonical connections while leaving the psalms’ original forms and voices visible.",
